<p>A widening war in the Middle East has damaged oil and gas production capacity in Saudi Arabia and Qatar and disrupted a key export corridor, putting fresh strain on global energy markets. The World Bank and IMF said the conflict is likely to weaken global growth as higher fuel costs ripple through economies, with countries that rely heavily on imported energy facing the most pressure. The shock is both regional and global: damage to energy infrastructure and tighter flows through the Strait of Hormuz have pushed up prices and made shipments of oil and liquefied natural gas harder to manage. Even so, international lenders point to a constructive path for governments: avoid locking in fuel subsidies they cannot afford, and focus instead on managing the economic fallout in ways that protect fiscal stability.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Flow losses: IMF Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva said the conflict has cut global daily oil flows by about 13% and LNG shipments by about 20%.</li><li>Broad spillover: World Bank President Ajay Banga said the war&#x27;s economic effects will spread far beyond the region, as higher energy costs and uncertainty work their way through trade and public budgets.</li><li>Lasting scars: Georgieva said infrastructure damage, supply disruptions, and weaker confidence will leave lasting economic &#x27;scars,&#x27; and that the IMF now expects to downgrade world growth even in its most hopeful scenario.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>World Bank: Ajay Banga&#x27;s main policy warning was about public finances — countries should avoid energy subsidies they cannot afford, because trying to shield consumers from the shock can put even more strain on government budgets. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/middle-east-war-to-cut-growth-deliver-cascading-impact-world-bank-chief-says'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>IMF: Kristalina Georgieva described the disruption as a large, global, and uneven negative supply shock that will hit countries differently depending on how much they rely on imported fuel. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/business/637989-iran-war-global-economy/'>RT</a>)</li><li>Financial Times analysis: The Financial Times argued that this war is not just a price story; damage to oil and gas infrastructure has also reduced physical production capacity in the Gulf. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/f7e61ecd-59cb-4be0-92c4-94198e60ba76'>Financial Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/f7e61ecd-59cb-4be0-92c4-94198e60ba76'>The damage wrought on the Middle East’s oil and gas supplies</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/middle-east-war-to-cut-growth-deliver-cascading-impact-world-bank-chief-says'>Middle East war to cut growth, deliver cascading impact, World Bank chief says</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/business/637989-iran-war-global-economy/'>Middle East war triggering global energy ‘shock’ – IMF</a> - rt.com</li></ul>